OminX
=====

OminX is a Debian 13 (Trixie) amd64 live desktop centered on KDE Plasma 6, with
an installer, core desktop apps, a Debian/Flatpak software center, Steam/Proton
support, Wine/Lutris, compressed zram memory, and per-game Feral GameMode.

OminX Aurora is an original dark visual system built from midnight surfaces,
sea-glass highlights, sky-blue light, and restrained violet accents. Its
floating, translucent Plasma panel brings together the OminX Launcher, virtual
desktops, running applications, system tray, clock, and Show Desktop control.
The launcher offers pinned apps, responsive app search, and session controls.
Dolphin opens in a folder-first details view with Places, breadcrumbs, tabs,
search, and mounted storage and network locations.

OminX Store presents app and game discovery, categories, Library, and Updates.
Real package and Flatpak actions are delegated to KDE Discover and its Debian
and Flatpak backends. OminX Settings organizes Plasma and native Linux controls
into Workspace, Devices, Connectivity, Appearance, Applications, Accounts,
Time & input, Gaming, Accessibility, Privacy & security, and Updates. OminX
Setup guides new users through connection status, app choices, and gaming tools;
optional software is not installed without an explicit user action.

Open the OminX Launcher with Meta (usually the Windows-logo key on a PC
keyboard). KDE shortcuts can be reviewed or changed in System Settings.

Start with the OminX Welcome application. Compatibility and performance notes
are in compatibility.md and performance.md. Full build instructions are in the
project README at:
https://github.com/ma19031435-eng/OminX-Linux

Legacy images now start a mandatory update prompt at login. It opens the
repository URL above so you can download the latest installable ISO.

Automated ISO builds include build-info.txt in this directory. It records the
source commit, GitHub Actions run ID, branch, and UTC build time for that image.

The base system and bundled applications retain their upstream licenses and
identities. OminX does not include Microsoft code or claim that every Windows
application is compatible. No universal 50% latency reduction is promised.
